      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sensō-ji, Tokyo’s oldest temple, honors the bodhisattva Kannon and sits at the heart of Asakusa.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asakusa",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Hanayashiki, opened over 150 years ago as a flower park, is Japan’s oldest amusement park still in operation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.japan-guide.com/e/e3004.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Tobu Asakusa Station, the northern terminus for trains to Nikko, houses an eight‑floor Matsuya department store inside its building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.japan-guide.com/e/e3004.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Sumida River borders Asakusa, and a waterbus pier just three minutes from the station lets visitors cruise to Odaiba and Toyosu.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        10,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.gotokyo.org/en/destinations/eastern-tokyo/asakusa/index.html",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Asakusa is part of Shitamachi, the low‑lying “old city” on the river that preserves a traditionally Japanese atmosphere.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asakusa",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Every spring, Asakusa hosts Sanja Matsuri, one of Tokyo’s largest festivals, where thousands gather to honor the three shrine founders.",
